\textbf{Chapter 3 -- System Requirements and Design}

\textbf{3.1 Project Scope, System Capabilities and Limitations}

\textbf{3.1.1 Project Scope}

CogniShield is an open-source, vendor-neutral, LLM-assisted network
automation and operations platform developed as a student project and
targeted at universities, small and medium enterprises and research
laboratories. The current project scope focuses primarily on Day-1 and
Day-2 network operations for mixed-vendor and legacy-oriented
environments, rather than full end-to-end autonomous provisioning. In
its current implementation, the system provides a web-based
controller-style workspace that integrates planned inventory management,
live topology visibility, knowledge-base-assisted AI support, Ansible
playbook generation, dry-run checking, approval-governed deployment,
compliance and drift functions, incident and security views, onboarding
support and configuration backup.

Within the automation workflow, the project scope centers on turning
natural-language configuration requests into reviewable and governed
Ansible-based change artifacts. This includes a prompt-driven interface
in Ansible Studio, preview and clarification flow for missing or
ambiguous parameters, schema- and context-based validation, playbook
generation, dry-run checking, approval queue handling and controlled
deployment to supported lab or permitted devices. The system also
supports related operational views such as topology inspection,
planned-versus-live context comparison, intent history, playbook
comparison, knowledge base management and backup-related workflows. For
onboarding, the scope includes generated bootstrap configuration and ZTP
publishing support, but not a full zero-touch autonomous onboarding
pipeline.

The project intentionally focuses on network operations and automation
concerns such as inventory, topology, playbook workflow, validation,
approval, monitoring support, compliance, incidents and configuration
handling. It does not aim to cover application lifecycle orchestration,
large-scale production high availability or fully autonomous closed-loop
network control. Likewise, the system should not be described as a full
SDN controller, a strict full intent-based networking platform or a
complete Day-0 to Day-2

autonomous controller. Although the platform includes AI assistance and
controller-style organization, the current implementation remains
centered on human-governed automation rather than autonomous execution
authority.

The design emphasizes demonstrability and reproducibility in an academic
and lab setting. The implementation is intended to be runnable on
accessible virtualized infrastructure and tested against environments
such as EVE-NG, planned inventory data and reproducible lab scenarios,
so that the system can be evaluated realistically within a student
project context without requiring expensive proprietary platforms or
specialized enterprise hardware.

\textbf{3.1.2 System Capabilities --- Module Description}

\textbf{Network Topology Visualization Module}

The Network Topology Visualization Module provides a browser-based
environment for representing and comparing the planned and live states
of the network. In the current implementation, this module functions
primarily as a visibility and comparison layer that helps users
understand network structure, inspect device relationships and observe
differences between intended design information and discovered
operational state.

The module maintains two complementary topology perspectives. The
planned topology is derived from the system's inventory and planned
network records, including device information, interface data, link
definitions and related structured network details. Where applicable,
NetBox-related inventory and planned-topology functions support this
planned view. This layer reflects the intended network design and
provides a structured reference for device roles, connectivity
expectations and managed assets.

The live topology is built from discovery-oriented operational data,
such as crawler results and live topology snapshots gathered from the
environment. In the currently delivered scope, this live layer is used
to show discovered nodes, links and operational relationships in a
browser-based dashboard environment. This allows users to inspect how
the network currently appears from the system's discovery and live-state
perspective.

A key capability of this module is support for planned-versus-live
awareness. By presenting both perspectives within the same platform, the
system helps users identify differences such as devices that exist in
one view but not the other, mismatched topology expectations, stale
inventory assumptions or operational drift between documented design and
discovered state. In the current implementation, this comparison is
surfaced mainly through topology views, dashboard context and related
inventory pages rather than through a fully autonomous reconciliation
engine.

The module is designed to remain accessible in student, lab and
SME-oriented environments. Its interface is lightweight and intended for
practical use in moderate-sized campus or lab topologies, allowing users
to inspect network structure without relying on heavy proprietary
controller platforms. Overall, this module strengthens operational
visibility by giving users a

clearer understanding of both what the network is expected to look like
and what the system is currently able to observe.

\textbf{Knowledge-Grounded LLM Playbook Generation Module}

The Knowledge-Grounded LLM Playbook Generation Module provides the
system's prompt-assisted automation workflow for converting
natural-language network requests into reviewable Ansible-based change
artifacts. In the current implementation, this module is centered around
Ansible Studio, where users can describe a requested network change in
plain language and progressively refine it into a structured and
governable automation task.

The workflow begins when a user submits a configuration request through
the prompt-driven interface. Instead of executing directly from the
prompt, the system first interprets the request and attempts to extract
a structured representation of the intended operation, including the
likely category, target devices and relevant parameters. When
information is incomplete, ambiguous or inconsistent with the planned
context, the system provides clarification and guided follow-up rather
than proceeding blindly.

To support this process, the module makes use of knowledge-grounded
context from the current system. This may include planned inventory
data, device and interface records, stored reference playbooks, schema
definitions and knowledge-base content managed through the platform.
These supporting sources help the system constrain generation and
improve relevance, especially in multi-vendor and legacy-oriented
workflows. In this sense, the module is not only prompt-driven but also
context-supported.

An important characteristic of the current implementation is that
playbook generation is hybrid rather than fully free-form. In many
supported flows, the LLM first helps interpret the request into
structured intent and the system then uses that result in a more
constrained generation path, such as reference-template filling or
validation-driven playbook preparation. In other cases, the LLM may
assist more directly in drafting the playbook. Regardless of the path
used, the result is still treated as a generated artifact that must pass
further checking before deployment.

The generated outputs are surfaced through the Ansible workflow,
allowing users to inspect the resulting playbook and continue into
dry-run and approval stages. For this reason, the

module should be understood not as an autonomous configuration engine,
but as an LLM-assisted and knowledge-grounded playbook preparation
workflow that helps users move from natural-language requests to
structured network automation in a more usable and controlled way.

\textbf{Playbook Validation \& Execution Module}

The Playbook Validation \& Execution Module provides the platform's main
safety and governance workflow for handling generated automation
artifacts before they are applied to devices. It functions as the
controlled operational layer that connects playbook preparation with
dry-run checking, approval, deployment and execution visibility.

After a playbook is generated, the system supports validation-oriented
handling before execution. In the current implementation, this includes
playbook checking, structured workflow gating and dry-run support
through Ansible check mode where applicable. The purpose of this stage
is to help users inspect whether the playbook is suitable for deployment
before any live configuration changes are attempted. Rather than
assuming that generated output is safe by default, the system requires
review and verification as part of the workflow.

A central capability of this module is the Check step, which allows
users to perform a dry-run style assessment before submission for
approval or deployment. The corresponding outputs are used to help users
understand what the playbook is likely to do and whether any issues must
be resolved first. This creates a more controlled operational flow than
directly applying generated changes.

Once a playbook has been checked, the module supports an
approval-governed deployment process. In the current exposed
implementation, the workflow includes sending the playbook for approval,
managing it through the Approval Queue and only allowing deployment
after the required review stage has been completed. This
approval-oriented structure is one of the key governance mechanisms of
the system and is especially important because CogniShield is designed
as a human-governed automation platform rather than an autonomous
executor.

After approval, the system supports controlled execution and surfaces
execution-related information through logs, results and associated job
records. The focus of the current implementation is on making the
deployment workflow understandable, reviewable and

operationally traceable. The module should therefore be described
primarily in terms of playbook review, dry-run checking, approval
workflow and controlled execution visibility, rather than as a fully
autonomous rollback and recovery engine.

Overall, this module ensures that prompt-assisted network automation
remains governed by validation, user review and explicit deployment
control rather than by unrestricted model output.

\textbf{Performance Metric Monitoring Module}

The Performance Metric Monitoring Module provides the platform's
operational monitoring view for observing network health and
performance-related indicators. In the current implementation, this
module is centered around frontend-visible monitoring pages that help
users inspect device and interface status, review active alerts and
observe performance trends in a practical dashboard environment.

The module presents monitoring-oriented information such as interface
state, traffic-related metrics, device resource indicators and alert
summaries where available through the current monitoring pipeline. Its
purpose is to give users a clearer view of network conditions and help
them identify abnormal or degraded behavior without relying solely on
manual command-line inspection.

A key role of this module is to support operational awareness before and
after configuration changes. By surfacing relevant performance
indicators and alert-related context, the module helps users assess
whether a network segment appears healthy, whether there are visible
issues requiring attention and whether recent changes may have affected
the observed state of the environment. In this sense, the module
complements the automation workflow by providing monitoring context
rather than by acting as a self-healing or autonomous remediation
engine.

The current implementation focuses on visibility, summarization and
inspection rather than unattended corrective action. It should therefore
be described as a performance monitoring and alert-awareness module, not
as a full incident-response or automated remediation subsystem. This
keeps the module aligned with the delivered frontend scope while still
reflecting its practical value within the overall platform.

Overall, the Performance Metric Monitoring Module strengthens
CogniShield by providing accessible monitoring support that helps users
understand current network behavior, correlate observed conditions with
planned or recent operational activity and work more confidently within
the platform's governed automation workflow.

\textbf{3.1.3 System Limitations}

CogniShield is developed with explicit technical, operational and
scope-related limitations that should be stated clearly so that the
project is evaluated according to what the current implementation
actually delivers.

\textbf{Multi-vendor and legacy-environment constraints:}

A major limitation arises from the type of environments targeted by the
project. CogniShield is designed for mixed-vendor and legacy-oriented
networks, which means many devices may rely on vendor-specific CLI
behavior, partial automation support, inconsistent module capabilities
and limited modern programmability interfaces. In such environments, it
is difficult to implement a uniform, fully autonomous end-to-end
automation workflow because different vendors and device families do not
always expose the same management abstractions, transaction models,
rollback behavior or context interfaces. This is one of the key reasons
the current system should not be described as a full SDN controller, a
strict full intent-based networking platform or a fully autonomous Day-0
to Day-2 control system. Instead, its automation is implemented
primarily through LLM-assisted, Ansible-centered, approval-governed
workflows that remain practical for legacy and heterogeneous
environments.

\textbf{Nature of the current automation workflow:}

The automation provided by CogniShield is not fully autonomous
automation that supports the entire network lifecycle from Day-0
onboarding to continuous deployment and operation like Software-Defined
Networking (SDN) controllers, but rather a governed Ansible-based
prompt-to-device execution workflow. In this context, Ansible automation
refers to the use of structured and repeatable playbooks to automate
network configuration validation and deployment tasks across target
devices in a consistent and manageable manner. Therefore, the system is
best understood as an LLM-assisted playbook-generation and
governed-execution platform, rather than a system that automatically
derives all network behavior and continuously enforces it without human
oversight. The automation strength of the current system lies in helping
users prepare, validate, and execute network changes more efficiently,
consistently, and safely through reusable playbooks and controlled
workflows across multiple network devices. By reducing the need for
manual device-by-device configuration, the

system also helps minimize human error and operational inconsistency
while improving deployment speed, configuration consistency, and
operational efficiency, rather than fully replacing network engineers or
providing fully autonomous network management.

\textbf{LLM limitations, generation risk and prompt dependence:}

Another major limitation arises from the use of large language models.
LLM outputs are probabilistic and may produce responses that are
plausible in form but incomplete, incorrect or operationally unsuitable.
In the context of CogniShield, model output must therefore not be
treated as authoritative network truth. The system treats generated
results as reviewable artifacts rather than direct executable
instructions. In practice, the LLM is used mainly to interpret
natural-language requests and assist playbook generation while the
surrounding system applies schema checks, planned-context checks,
dry-run support and approval workflow to reduce risk.

The quality of results also depends heavily on the style and clarity of
the user prompt. The current workflow works best when prompts are
operationally scoped and reasonably specific, for example requests such
as configuring VLANs, interfaces, static routes, ACLs, QoS or OSPF on
named devices. More abstract or business-level requests may require
clarification and very broad prompts may not reliably produce complete
automation artifacts in one pass. As a result, users should expect
CogniShield to work most effectively with device-scoped,
operation-oriented prompts rather than vague high-level policy
statements. Human oversight remains necessary because even a well-formed
prompt may still lead to incomplete or suboptimal generation.

\textbf{Supported operation and orchestration limits:}

The current implementation does not provide unlimited operational
coverage across all network tasks. It is centered on selected automation
categories and their associated workflow support, rather than universal
handling of every possible network operation. Complex multi-step
requests, highly coupled cross-device changes or prompts involving
multiple simultaneous operations may still require clarification,
decomposition or manual refinement. This means that the system should
not be expected to reliably transform every complex prompt into a fully
complete playbook without additional user guidance.

\textbf{Hardware and model constraints:}

Hardware and runtime constraints also define the project's operational
limits. Running large on-premise foundation models typically requires
substantial GPU resources and additional deployment complexity, which
are outside the normal scope of a university student project. For this
reason, the implementation is designed around accessible model options
such as hosted LLM APIs or lightweight local models that can operate in
modest lab environments. Similarly, the computing resources available in
student machines, virtual machines and EVE-NG-based lab setups impose
practical constraints on the size and complexity of the environments
that can be demonstrated and tested. As a result, the project is
optimized for manageable and reproducible lab-scale deployments rather
than large production-scale environments.

\textbf{Partial dry-run and execution predictability:}

Another limitation concerns dry-run support. Although CogniShield
provides a check-oriented workflow and can use Ansible check mode where
applicable, not all modules, vendor behaviors or playbook actions can be
simulated with equal completeness. This means dry-run outputs may be
partial for some operations and users must still interpret the results
carefully before deployment. The system reduces risk through workflow
control and review, but dry-run support should not be interpreted as a
guarantee of complete execution predictability for every task.

\textbf{Dependence on context quality and state consistency:}

The quality of generated outputs depends significantly on the quality of
the supporting context available to the system. Planned inventory
records, topology information, interface data, stored references and
knowledge-base content all influence how accurately a request can be
interpreted and prepared. If this context is incomplete, outdated,
inconsistent or missing, the resulting playbook workflow may require
more clarification or may produce less reliable intermediate outputs. In
addition, the platform does not yet fully eliminate state-authority
tensions between LLM interpretation, planned inventory, live
observations and user overrides. Therefore, CogniShield performs best
when the surrounding inventory and contextual records are reasonably
maintained.

\textbf{Partial Day-0 and non-autonomous onboarding scope:}

The system also has limitations in onboarding and lifecycle automation.
The current implementation supports onboarding assistance, generated
bootstrap configuration and related publishing workflows, but it does
not yet provide a fully autonomous zero-touch onboarding process in
which newly powered devices automatically join, authenticate, retrieve
initial management settings, receive the required bootstrap
configuration and transition seamlessly into full operational control.
This limitation is influenced not only by the mixed-vendor and legacy
nature of the target environment, but also by the characteristics of the
testing platform used in this project.

In this project, EVE-NG is used as the main laboratory environment,
where many network devices are virtualized images rather than full
physical hardware implementations. As a result, some virtual switch or
router images do not faithfully reproduce the complete Day-0 bootstrap
behavior available on actual devices. For example, certain images may
not fully support automatic DHCP-based onboarding flows, automatic
retrieval of configuration files from a bootstrap server or legacy
vendor-specific mechanisms such as Cisco AutoInstall-style startup
provisioning. In other cases, management interface naming and early boot
behavior may differ from real hardware, making automated zero-touch
registration and initial configuration delivery difficult to demonstrate
consistently.

Because of these constraints, the current onboarding workflow is
implemented in a more practical and controlled manner: the system
generates bootstrap or zero-day configuration artifacts and supports
onboarding-related publishing workflows, but the final application of
those initial configurations may still require manual or semi-manual
steps in the lab environment. Therefore, CogniShield should not yet be
described as a fully autonomous Day-0 onboarding platform. Its present
onboarding capability is better understood as assisted bootstrap
generation and onboarding support, shaped by both the realities of
mixed-vendor legacy environments and the limitations of virtualized
testbed behavior in EVE-NG.

\textbf{Architectural and deployment-model limitations:}

The current system is intentionally designed for mixed-vendor and
legacy-oriented network environments, which are common in SMEs, student
laboratories and research settings because these environments typically
rely on equipment that is acquired incrementally and under budget
constraints. In practice, such devices often expose heterogeneous
management methods, uneven automation support, vendor-specific CLI
behavior and inconsistent support for modern controller-driven
abstractions. Because of this heterogeneity, CogniShield does not
implement a single dominant control plane that can continuously program
and reconcile all devices in the manner expected of a full SDN
controller. Likewise, it should not be described as a strict full
intent-based networking platform, because the current implementation
does not yet provide deterministic end-to-end realization of all
high-level network goals through continuous verification and autonomous
policy reconciliation. Instead, the system uses LLM-assisted,
Ansible-centered and approval-governed automation workflows, where
natural-language requests are interpreted into structured change
artifacts that may still require clarification, schema validation,
planned-context checking, dry-run assessment and human approval before
execution. This makes CogniShield more accurately positioned as a
practical automation platform for heterogeneous and resource-constrained
environments rather than as a full SDN or fully realized IBN system.

\textbf{Scope and scale limits:}

Finally, the system is intentionally scoped for campus-style, SME,
educational and lab-oriented environments. Extremely large carrier
networks, hyperscale cloud fabrics, multi-region enterprise operations
and highly complex cross-domain orchestration are outside the project
boundaries. Likewise, the currently supported automation workflows,
schema coverage and playbook patterns are intentionally selective rather
than exhaustive. The goal of the project is to demonstrate a practical
and governed LLM-assisted network automation platform within a realistic
and manageable scope, not to claim universal coverage across all network
environments and operational scenarios.

