\textbf{3.2.3 SWOT Analysis}

\textbf{Strengths}

CogniShield offers several important strengths within the scope of its
current implementation. First, it is built by combining free and
open-source components into a more centralized management and automation
platform, which improves accessibility and reduces dependence on
expensive proprietary ecosystems. This makes it more suitable for
universities, research environments, SMEs and individual learners who
may not have access to commercial enterprise controllers. Because the
platform is open and modifiable, it also encourages community-based
development, experimentation and improvement, allowing users to study,
adapt and extend the system more freely. Second, it is intended for
mixed-vendor and legacy-oriented environments, making it more practical
for settings where users must work with heterogeneous devices rather
than a single tightly controlled vendor ecosystem. Third, its main
automation value lies in a governed LLM-assisted workflow that helps
users move from natural-language configuration requests to reviewable
Ansible-based playbooks through prompt interpretation, context support,
dry-run checking, approval handling and controlled execution. This helps
reduce manual effort while preserving human oversight. In addition, the
platform brings together multiple useful operational views in one
workspace, including topology and inventory visibility, Ansible Studio,
approval workflow, knowledge base support, monitoring-related views,
compliance and governance support, onboarding support and configuration
backup. This integrated and accessible structure is a practical strength
for academic, lab and cost-sensitive environments.

\textbf{Weaknesses}

The primary limitation of CogniShield remains its dependence on
LLM-assisted generation within a controlled but still imperfect
workflow. LLM outputs may be incomplete, incorrect or operationally
unsuitable, especially when prompts are vague, context is missing or
requests are too broad or complex. As a result, the system cannot safely
operate as an autonomous executor and still depends heavily on review,
checking and approval. Another weakness is that the current platform
does not yet provide full autonomous Day-0 to Day-2 lifecycle control,
nor does it deliver the deterministic policy reconciliation expected of
a full SDN or strict full intent-based networking platform.

The system is also affected by the limitations of its testing
environment, particularly the use of EVE-NG with limited RAM and CPU
resources. Because the lab environment relies heavily on virtualized
device images, some devices only provide basic or partial behavior
compared with real production hardware. Certain boot-time capabilities
that may exist on physical devices, such as legacy AutoInstall-style
provisioning, automatic DHCP-based bootstrap behavior or other
vendor-specific Day-0 processes, may not be fully present or
reproducible in the virtual images used for testing. This restricts how
far fully autonomous Day-0 onboarding can be demonstrated in practice.
In addition, some hardware-dependent behavior found in real physical
switches, especially ASIC-assisted packet forwarding characteristics, is
only simulated in the lab environment and may not fully reflect actual
hardware performance or implementation details.

Furthermore, the project depends on the integration of multiple
supporting components such as inventory, topology, monitoring, knowledge
base and Ansible workflow, which increases design and maintenance
complexity. For demonstration purposes, the test environment is
intentionally kept manageable, often using only one or two supporting
devices around the main workflow to prove that the platform operates as
intended. While this is sufficient for academic demonstration and
controlled validation, it also means that the scale, hardware fidelity
and operational diversity of the environment remain lower than those of
a full real-world production deployment.

\textbf{Opportunities}

CogniShield is well positioned to address a real accessibility gap in
network automation. Many commercial platforms are powerful but remain
costly, proprietary and less practical for students, researchers and
SMEs. This creates an opportunity for CogniShield to serve as a
lower-cost and more open platform for learning, experimentation and
controlled network automation in lab and mixed-vendor settings. There is
also a broader opportunity in the growing interest in LLM-assisted
operational workflows, especially where users want more usable
automation interfaces without abandoning governance and review. Because
CogniShield combines prompt-based interaction with Ansible workflow,
approval handling and contextual support, it can serve as a practical
bridge between traditional manual automation and more advanced
AI-assisted operations. The platform also has room to expand

incrementally in areas such as stronger onboarding support, broader
operation coverage, more mature context integration and tighter
validation logic.

\textbf{Threats}

The project faces strong competition from highly integrated proprietary
platforms that already provide mature operational tooling, enterprise
support and increasingly sophisticated AI-assisted features. As these
platforms continue to incorporate conversational interfaces, AI
assistance and broader orchestration capabilities, the relative novelty
of LLM-assisted interaction may become less distinctive over time.
Another threat is the inherent risk associated with LLM-based
generation, where if the surrounding context is incomplete, outdated or
misinterpreted, the resulting automation artifact may still be
inaccurate or unsafe despite the presence of validation steps. In
mixed-vendor and legacy environments, inconsistent device behavior,
limited programmability and uneven module support also create practical
risks that can reduce reliability and make automation harder to
generalize. Finally, because the system is developed within a
student-project and lab-oriented scope, there is a threat that
evaluators or users may compare it unfairly against full-scale
enterprise controllers unless its intended scope is stated clearly.
\end{quote}

\textbf{3.3 Development Methodology---Hybrid SDLC (Waterfall +}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Agile)}
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=4.25in,height=2.55278in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image6.png}

\begin{quote}
The CogniShield project adopts a \textbf{Hybrid Software Development
Life Cycle (SDLC)} that combines Waterfall and Agile practices. This
approach is more suitable than a pure Waterfall or pure Agile model
because the project contains two different development characteristics.
On one hand, it requires a structured academic foundation, including
literature review, scope definition, requirements analysis,
architectural planning, risk assessment and formal documentation. On the
other hand, several parts of the implemented system, especially the
frontend workflows, LLM-assisted playbook generation flow, topology
presentation and Ansible-based approval workflow, required iterative
refinement during development. For this reason, Waterfall elements were
used to define the overall project direction and system boundaries while
Agile elements were used to incrementally build, test and improve the
implemented modules. (GeeksforGeeks, 2025).

The Waterfall aspect of the methodology was applied mainly during the
early and control-oriented stages of the project. These include defining
the research problem, identifying the project scope, studying relevant
background materials, determining the module structure, documenting
system requirements, planning the Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) and
preparing the Gantt chart. This structured approach was important
because CogniShield deals with network automation workflows that must be
governed carefully. Even though the current implementation is not a full
SDN controller, not a strict full intent-based networking platform and
not a fully autonomous closed-loop system, it still handles
configuration-related workflows that require clear scope control,
validation logic and

approval-based execution. As a result, the project benefited from having
stable planning and clear stage boundaries before large-scale
implementation work began. (Gazala, 2025).

The Agile aspect of the methodology was applied during implementation
and refinement. In practice, the system was developed in module-oriented
iterations, where each major capability was designed, implemented,
tested, reviewed and adjusted based on the behavior of the integrated
platform. This was especially necessary for the current frontend-visible
\end{quote}

