\begin{quote}
Generation, Playbook Validation and Execution and Performance Metric
Monitoring. These modules could not be implemented effectively through a
purely linear one-pass workflow because their final behavior depended on
real integration outcomes, such as how prompt interpretation behaved in
Ansible Studio, how planned and live topology data could actually be
surfaced, how approval and dry-run workflows interacted in practice and
how the monitoring pages could present usable operational context.

This hybrid methodology also reflects the realities of the project
environment. CogniShield is developed as an individual Final Year
Project in a lab-oriented setting, with limited hardware resources,
mixed-vendor and legacy-oriented assumptions and EVE-NG-based testing
constraints. Under these conditions, iterative implementation was
necessary to adapt the design to what could be demonstrated reliably
while structured documentation remained necessary for supervision,
reporting and evaluation. Therefore, the chosen Hybrid SDLC provides a
balanced development model: it preserves the discipline of formal
planning while allowing controlled iteration where the actual
implementation required refinement.

Overall, the Hybrid SDLC supports CogniShield more accurately than a
pure Waterfall model. It reflects how the project was actually
developed: first through structured planning and documentation, then
through incremental implementation, integration, testing and revision of
the system's visible operational workflows.

\textbf{3.3.1 Planning}

The Planning phase forms the foundation of the CogniShield project by
defining the project direction, implementation scope, technical
boundaries and practical constraints. Since the
