\begin{quote}
approval-governed execution and monitoring support, careful planning was
necessary to ensure that the project remained realistic, demonstrable
and suitable for a student project environment.

The first activity in this phase involved reviewing academic literature,
technical documentation and platform references related to campus-style
networks, SME network constraints, multi-vendor operations, open-source
network automation, LLM-assisted workflows, Ansible-based orchestration,
topology and inventory visibility and performance monitoring. This
review was important for establishing the technical background of the
project and for positioning CogniShield correctly. In particular, it
helped distinguish the project from full SDN controllers and strict
intent-based networking platforms and instead define it more accurately
as an LLM-assisted, Ansible-centered, approval-governed network
automation and operations platform.

The planning stage also examined the tools and environments that would
shape the implementation, including planned inventory records, live
topology discovery workflows, Ansible automation, knowledge-base support
and EVE-NG laboratory testing. Special attention was given to
mixed-vendor and legacy-oriented environments, since these better
reflect the conditions faced by students, researchers and SMEs. This
helped ensure that the project scope remained practical and did not
assume uniform controller-driven automation across all devices.

Another key planning activity was defining the main implementation
modules. The system was organized around four practical and demonstrable
areas: Network Topology Visualization, Knowledge-Grounded LLM Playbook
Generation, Playbook Validation, Approval and Execution and Performance
Metric Monitoring. This module-based structure helped keep the
development manageable while ensuring that each part contributed
directly to the visible platform.

Risk assessment was also carried out during this phase. Important
concerns included ambiguous prompts, incorrect generated playbooks,
incomplete context, mixed-vendor inconsistencies, partial dry-run
coverage, credential handling and the limitations of the EVE-NG test
environment. These considerations influenced the scope of the project
and helped avoid overstating capabilities such as full Day-0 autonomy or
fully autonomous network control.

After clarifying the scope and constraints, a Work Breakdown Structure
(WBS) and Gantt chart were prepared to organize the development process
into planning, analysis, design, implementation, integration and testing
phases. Finally, practical success criteria were defined based on
whether the system could reliably support its intended visible
workflows, including playbook generation, clarification, checking,
approval, deployment, topology viewing and performance monitoring.
Through this planning process, the project was able to proceed with a
clearer, more realistic and more defensible implementation direction.

\textbf{3.3.2 Analysis}

The Analysis phase converts the planning outcomes into clear, testable
and traceable system requirements. For CogniShield, this stage was used
to transform findings from literature, technical references and early
planning into a formal Software Requirements Specification (SRS) and a
prioritised feature set that fits the available time, hardware resources
and laboratory constraints (TutorialsCampus, n.d.).

The first activity in this phase was to identify the functional
requirements based on the actual scope of the current implementation.
Rather than analysing the system as a fully autonomous controller, the
requirements were defined around the platform's delivered workflows.
These include submitting a scoped natural-language configuration request
in Ansible Studio, reviewing parsed requests and clarification prompts,
generating a reviewable Ansible playbook, running a check-mode or
dry-run process where supported, submitting a playbook for approval,
deploying an approved playbook, viewing planned and live topology,
inspecting inventory-linked device information and reviewing
performance-related monitoring pages. These requirements were then
formalised into use cases and functional requirement entries for later
testing and evaluation (Letaw, 2024).

Non-functional requirements were also analysed because performance,
usability, reliability, safety and auditability are important for a
network automation platform, even in a student lab
\end{quote}

