\begin{quote}
playbook-generation turnaround, responsiveness of topology and
monitoring views and visibility of approval and execution records.
Minimum audit information such as generated playbook artefacts, approval
status, execution-related logs and timestamps was also identified so
that system actions remain reviewable and traceable (GeeksforGeeks,
2025c).

The analysis phase also identified the technical interfaces and data
relationships that the system must support. Because CogniShield is
designed for mixed-vendor and legacy-oriented environments, the analysis
did not assume a uniform controller-driven management model across all
devices. Instead, the requirements were defined around practical
interfaces such as planned inventory records, topology and
discovery-derived data, knowledge-base content, monitoring-related
inputs and Ansible-centered execution workflows. UML artefacts such as
use case diagrams, activity diagrams, sequence diagrams and a
lightweight data model were

prepared to describe these workflows and system records more clearly
(What Is Unified Modeling Language (UML)?, n.d.).

Because the project uses LLM-assisted generation, the analysis also
identified the contextual sources that support playbook generation.
These include planned inventory records, device and interface data,
schema definitions, stored reference playbooks and knowledge-base
content. In the current implementation, this context is treated as
bounded support for improving playbook quality rather than as a fully
autonomous knowledge layer. Security and threat considerations were also
analysed, including ambiguous prompts, unsafe generated output,
incomplete context, credential misuse and the risks of applying changes
too quickly in mixed-vendor environments. To reduce these risks, the
analysis defined requirements for clarification, constrained generation
where applicable, schema and context checking, dry-run support,
approval-based deployment and audit logging (Chauhan \& Chauhan, 2025).

Practical constraints and assumptions were also recorded explicitly in
this phase so that the later design remained realistic. These included
the hardware limitations of the EVE-NG test environment, the use of
hosted LLM APIs or lightweight local models instead of large
GPU-dependent models and the limited hardware resources available in a
student-lab setting. The analysis also acknowledged that many lab
devices are virtualized images with only partial hardware fidelity. As a
result, some real Day-0 behaviors, such as legacy AutoInstall-style
provisioning, DHCP-based bootstrap behavior and ASIC-dependent
forwarding characteristics, cannot be reproduced fully in the testing
environment. For this reason, the project scope was analysed around
demonstrable and governable workflows rather than full end-to-end
autonomous lifecycle control. Recording these assumptions early helped
keep the later design and implementation aligned with what could be
implemented and defended reliably within the project environment.

Finally, a requirements-to-test traceability matrix was prepared to link
each requirement to its related module and acceptance test. Practical
constraints were also recorded, including the limited hardware resources
of the EVE-NG environment, the use of hosted LLM APIs or lightweight
local models and the partial hardware fidelity of virtualized devices.
This helped keep the later design and implementation aligned with what
could be implemented and defended reliably within the project
environment (Requirements Traceability Matrix: Definition, Benefits and
Examples \textbar{} Perforce Software, n.d.).

