\begin{quote}
\textbf{3.3.2.3 Non-Functional Requirements}

Non-functional requirements define the quality attributes, operational
constraints and performance expectations of CogniShield beyond its
functional capabilities. These requirements are important because the
system works with network configuration workflows, planned and live
topology data, approval-governed automation and LLM-assisted playbook
generation, where reliability, safety, usability and traceability must
be considered carefully within the scope of a student laboratory
environment (GeeksforGeeks, 2025a). The following non-functional
requirements are defined to remain measurable, practical and aligned
with the current existing implementation.

1. \textbf{Performance}

\textbf{●\hspace{0pt} Request and Playbook Workflow Responsiveness:} The
system should respond to typical natural-language requests within a
practical interactive time under normal laboratory conditions, so that
users can preview, generate and review playbooks without excessive
delay.

\textbf{●\hspace{0pt} Topology View Responsiveness:} Planned, live and
comparison topology views should load and update in a usable time frame
for lab-scale topologies, with manual refresh and available live update
support where applicable.

\textbf{●\hspace{0pt} Monitoring View Responsiveness:} The Performance
page should load device summaries, interface metrics and alert
information in a reasonable time for normal lab usage.

\textbf{●\hspace{0pt} Check Workflow Responsiveness:} The playbook check
or dry-run workflow should return results within a practical time frame
suitable for review before approval or deployment.

2. \textbf{Security and Governance}

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Credential Handling:} Device credentials, API
tokens and related sensitive values should be handled through protected
credential-management mechanisms and should not be exposed directly
through normal user workflows.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Access Control:} Authentication and role-based
access control should be enforced so that only authorized users can
submit, approve, reject or deploy configuration changes.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Approval-Governed Execution:} The system should
require explicit review and approval before executing controlled
configuration changes through the playbook deployment workflow.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{LLM Output Safety Controls:} Generated playbooks
should pass through clarification, supporting-context checks, check-mode
review where supported and approval workflow before live deployment,
reducing the risk of unsafe or unsuitable model output being applied
directly to devices.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Auditability:} Generated playbooks, approval
decisions, execution-related results and timestamps should remain
reviewable through the platform so that configuration actions can be
traced during testing and evaluation.

3. \textbf{Maintainability}

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Modular Structure:} The main platform workflows,
including topology visualization, LLM-assisted playbook generation,
playbook validation and execution and performance monitoring, should
remain sufficiently modular so that they can be updated and debugged
without requiring major redesign.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Documentation:} The project should include clear
module descriptions, report-level documentation and code comments where
necessary so that future students or researchers can understand and
extend the system.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Version Control:} Source code, maintained
templates and core project artefacts should be managed in a Git
repository to support change tracking and controlled development.

4. \textbf{Usability and Operator Experience}

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Guided Workflow Usability:} The interface should
provide clear workflow guidance for major tasks such as request
submission, clarification, playbook review, checking, approval,
deployment, topology viewing and performance monitoring.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Readable Feedback:} Validation results, check
outputs, warnings, approval states and execution results should be
presented in a clear and understandable way so that users can interpret
the workflow without unnecessary confusion.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Learnability:} A new operator in a laboratory
setting should be able to perform basic tasks such as submitting a
request, reviewing a generated playbook, running a check and viewing
topology or monitoring pages after brief guided instruction.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Interface Consistency:} The main frontend views
should maintain consistent navigation and labeling so that users can
move between Ansible Studio, the topology view, the approval queue and
the monitoring pages with minimal relearning.

\textbf{3.3.3 Design}

The design of CogniShield focuses on building a reliable and
well-structured workflow that supports natural-language request
submission, LLM-assisted Ansible playbook generation, playbook checking,
approval-governed execution, topology visibility, and performance
monitoring. Since the goal of the project is to support network
automation in a controlled and practical way, the design emphasizes
clear process flow, validation and review steps, and sufficient
traceability throughout the workflow. Rather than acting as a fully
autonomous controller, the current implementation is designed around
human-governed automation, where generated playbooks are reviewed,
checked, approved, and then deployed through a controlled execution
process. The system should therefore be understood as an LLM-assisted,
Ansible-centered operations platform, not as a full SDN controller, a
strict Intent-Based Networking platform, or a closed-loop autonomous
remediation system.

Before describing the detailed flows for request submission, playbook
generation, checking, approval, deployment, topology viewing, and
monitoring support, it is helpful to first present the overall system
workflow. By understanding this overall interaction, it becomes easier
to see how the main components of the platform, such as request input,
supporting context, playbook generation, topology data, approval
handling, and execution-related views, work together. The overall design
is then elaborated through sequence diagrams, activity diagrams, use
case diagrams, and related design artefacts that describe the major
operational scenarios of the current implementation. Figure 8 focuses on
the core operational workflows of the platform, while supporting
capabilities such as Device Inventory, Knowledge Base management,
Onboarding, Template Studio, Global Service Profiles, and Configuration
Backup are treated as auxiliary platform functions rather than separate
core modules in this design flow.

Figure 8 below illustrates the overall workflow of CogniShield as an
integrated platform and provides a clearer picture of how its main
visible components interact in the current implementation.

\includegraphics[width=7.21944in,height=7.46805in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image11.png}
\end{quote}

\emph{Figure 8: Overall Workflow of CogniShield}

\begin{quote}
The system flow begins when an engineer submits a natural-language
network change request through Ansible Studio. Once received, the system
parses the request and attempts to identify the intended operation,
target devices, and key parameters. If important information is missing,
ambiguous, or misinterpreted, the system requests clarification, for
example by asking the user to confirm target devices from the planned
inventory or to provide missing interface- or configuration-related
values. This makes the workflow more controlled than direct free-form
prompt-to-device execution.

After the request is clarified, the system prepares the supporting
context used for playbook generation. In the current implementation,
this context is based mainly on planned inventory records, device and
interface information, intent schemas, stored reference playbooks, and
curated knowledge-base content where applicable. Planned inventory is
managed through the application's local inventory layer and can be
synchronized with NetBox where configured. Using this information, the
platform generates a reviewable Ansible playbook through a constrained
workflow that may combine reference-based generation and LLM-assisted
drafting, depending on the request type and available support. This
means the design does not rely on unrestricted free-form LLM output for
every operation.

Once a playbook is generated, the engineer reviews it in the interface
before continuing. The next stage is the Check workflow, where the
system runs Ansible check mode or dry-run handling where supported and
returns the resulting summary and diff output. This allows the engineer
to inspect the expected effect of the playbook before submitting it for
approval. After that, the playbook can be sent into the approval
workflow, reviewed in the Approval Queue, and either approved or
rejected by an authorized reviewer. If approved, the playbook can then
be deployed through the controlled execution workflow, where execution
logs and job results are recorded and made visible to the user. In the
lab environment, actual playbook execution may be delegated to a remote
Linux Ansible runner, but the user-facing workflow remains the same.

In addition to this playbook workflow, the platform includes supporting
operational views that help the user work with the network more
effectively. These include planned and live topology visualization,
comparison between both views, device and connection detail inspection,
and performance monitoring pages that show device summaries, interface
health, alert status, and time-series trends. Together, these views
provide additional operational context for understanding the network
before and after configuration changes. Supporting

platform capabilities such as Device Inventory, Knowledge Base,
Onboarding, Template Studio, Global Service Profiles, and Configuration
Backup further strengthen the usability of the platform, even though
they are treated as auxiliary capabilities rather than the four main
core modules in the present design structure.

The user interface is designed to keep the main workflows clear and
manageable. Ansible Studio serves as the main workspace for request
submission, parsed-request review, clarification, generated playbook
review, refinement, checking, and deployment preparation. The topology
page supports planned, live, and comparison views, while the Performance
page presents monitoring information in a more readable dashboard form.
The Approval Queue provides a dedicated place for review decisions, and
the Knowledge Base supports the document and reference layer used by the
platform. In addition, the Device Inventory page supports planned device
and interface management, while the other supporting pages provide
onboarding, template, and backup-related functionality that complements
the core workflow.

From a data perspective, the design keeps a practical separation between
planned network records, generated playbook artefacts, job-related
workflow data, knowledge-support data, and monitoring data. Planned
inventory and topology information provide the design-side reference
used in multiple workflows, while generated playbooks, approval states,
and execution records support traceability across the automation
pipeline. Monitoring-related data is handled separately so that
performance pages can retrieve current and historical metrics without
being mixed directly into the playbook workflow. Knowledge-base
documents and reference materials are also maintained separately to
support bounded retrieval during playbook generation. This structure
helps maintain a clear distinction between planning context, automation
artefacts, supporting knowledge, and operational observations.

Overall, the design ensures that CogniShield behaves in a controlled and
understandable way within its current scope. Rather than operating as a
fully autonomous controller, the platform is designed around
LLM-assisted, approval-governed network automation, supported by
topology visibility, planned inventory context, knowledge-base
assistance and monitoring views. This makes it suitable for laboratory
demonstration, guided experimentation, and practical mixed-vendor
network automation workflows without overstating capabilities that are
outside the current implementation.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=7.44861in,height=5.55278in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image12.png}

\emph{Figure 9: Request Submission and Playbook Generation Sequence
Diagram}

\includegraphics[width=7.15694in,height=5.53194in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image13.png}

\emph{Figure 10: Check, Approval, Validation and Execution Sequence
Diagram}

\includegraphics[width=7.13472in,height=5.36528in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image14.png}

\emph{Figure 11: Topology and Performance Monitoring Sequence Diagram}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{GUI Behaviour}

The GUI is designed to keep the main workflows clear and manageable by
showing only the information needed for the current task. The platform
provides a dashboard-style entry point together with dedicated pages for
topology viewing, device inventory, performance monitoring, Ansible
Studio, the approval queue, knowledge-base management, onboarding
support, template handling and configuration backup. In the current
implementation, Ansible Studio serves as the main workspace for
natural-language request submission, request preview, clarification,
generated playbook review, playbook checking, approval submission and
deployment preparation. The topology page supports planned, live and
comparison views while the Performance page presents device summaries,
interface health, alert information and time-series charts in a readable
format. The Approval Queue provides a separate review workflow for
approving or rejecting playbooks and the Knowledge Base supports
document and reference management for the LLM-assisted workflow.
Overall, the interface is designed to support guided, approval-governed
network automation rather than unrestricted direct execution.

\textbf{Database Schema and Data Stores}

CogniShield keeps different types of data in separate storage layers so
that planning context, automation artefacts and monitoring information
remain organized. In the current implementation, SQLite serves as the
main relational data store for application and workflow data. This
relational layer is used to support records related to planned
inventory, generated playbooks, approval states, execution-related
workflow information, user-facing operational data and other persistent
platform artefacts that need to remain queryable and traceable across
the system.

Planned inventory and topology data are maintained through the
inventory-related system components, with NetBox used as a primary
reference source where applicable. Monitoring and time-series data are
handled separately through the monitoring stack for efficient retrieval
of device and interface metrics. Knowledge-base documents and related
retrieval-support artefacts are also managed separately to support the
LLM-assisted workflow. This separation helps the system maintain a clear
distinction between planned network records, generated automation
outputs, relational workflow data and operational monitoring data while
keeping the current implementation practical for a student laboratory
environment.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=5.875in,height=9.15694in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image15.png}

\emph{Figure 12: Entity-Relationship Diagram}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{3.3.4 Implementation}

The implementation phase translates the design into a working and
reproducible platform that can be demonstrated in a student laboratory
and extended in future work. In the current implementation, CogniShield
uses a Python-first backend built with FastAPI and Uvicorn, together
with a React + TypeScript + Vite frontend, because this combination
supports rapid development, network-oriented integration and responsive
user interaction (The Python Standard Library, n.d.; FastAPI, n.d.;
Quick Start - React, n.d.). The backend provides the main API, workflow
logic and live update endpoints while the frontend provides a
single-page workspace for topology viewing, device inventory, Ansible
Studio, approval handling, knowledge-base support, onboarding, template
management, configuration backup and performance monitoring.

To connect the high-level design with a demonstrable lab deployment, the
low-level implementation is arranged as a small set of practical runtime
components. These include the main application, planned inventory and
topology support, the monitoring stack, the knowledge-base layer, the
Ansible execution environment and the EVE-NG network simulator. In the
student laboratory, these components are distributed across multiple
virtual machines hosted on Proxmox. For example, the lab layout
separates the EVE-NG environment, NetBox and monitoring services and the
Linux-based Ansible runner or optional Ollama service into different VMs
while the main CogniShield application can run on the host machine or a
dedicated VM. Docker Compose is used where practical to make local
deployment more repeatable, but the overall setup is intended as a
manageable laboratory environment rather than a production-scale
orchestration platform.

\includegraphics[width=9.69722in,height=5.72917in]{vertopal_ecf677e2c67e42e3980383773f2ef664/media/image16.png}
\end{quote}

\emph{Figure 13: Low-Level CogniShield Architecture Diagram}

\begin{quote}
At the center of the current implementation is the LLM-assisted Ansible
workflow. The process begins in Ansible Studio, where a user submits a
natural-language network change request. The backend then parses the
request, identifies the likely action and targets and asks for
clarification when important information is missing or ambiguous. After
that, the system prepares the supporting context used for playbook
generation. In the current implementation, this context is based mainly
on planned inventory records, device and interface data, stored
reference playbooks, intent schemas and curated knowledge-base content
where applicable. This keeps the workflow bounded and practical, rather
than depending on unrestricted retrieval or a full SDN-state model for
every request.

Playbook generation is implemented as a constrained and reviewable
workflow rather than unrestricted direct model output. The system
supports Gemini and Ollama as LLM providers while knowledge retrieval is
supported through ChromaDB for curated reference material and uploaded
documents. Once a playbook is generated, it is shown to the user for
review. The system then supports a Check workflow using Ansible check
mode or dry-run handling where supported. After that, the playbook can
be submitted to the Approval Queue, reviewed and either approved or
rejected. If approved, it can be deployed through the controlled
execution workflow. Because Ansible control-node support is limited on
Windows, the current implementation also supports a remote Linux Ansible
runner VM, allowing the main application to keep the same user workflow
while delegating playbook execution to a more suitable environment.

The implementation also includes both planned and live topology
visualization. Planned topology is derived from planned inventory and
topology-related records, including NetBox-synchronized information
where available. Live topology is built through the discovery pipeline,
which uses vendor-aware SSH drivers and LLDP/CDP-style neighbor
collection to construct the current topology view. The topology page
supports planned, live and comparison modes so that users can inspect
network structure and observe visible differences between documented and
discovered state. In parallel, the Performance page provides device
summaries, interface health, active alert status and time-series trends
using the monitoring stack.

From the frontend perspective, the interface is implemented with tools
that match these workflows directly. D3.js is used for topology
visualization, Monaco Editor is used for playbook and template editing
and Recharts is used for performance charts. WebSocket

support is used for live notifications, telemetry updates and
execution-related status visibility where applicable. This allows the
interface to remain interactive while the backend continues handling
longer-running network operations.

For persistent data, the current implementation uses SQLite as the main
relational data store. This local relational layer is used for
application and workflow records such as inventory-linked data, playbook
jobs, approvals, logs and other persistent artefacts. Monitoring and
time-series data are handled separately through InfluxDB, with Telegraf
used for SNMP-based metric collection and Prometheus/Grafana integrated
where available in the wider lab environment. Knowledge-base vector data
is stored separately through the ChromaDB persistence layer. This
separation keeps planned records, automation artefacts and monitoring
data organized while remaining suitable for a student laboratory
deployment.

The frontend is designed to be simple and easy to use. Network
topologies are shown using D3.js to make the connections and layout
clear (Holtz, n.d.). The playbook editor uses Monaco Editor or Ace
Editor so YAML and CLI commands are highlighted and checked for errors
(\emph{Monaco Editor}, n.d.). Live telemetry and execution logs are
streamed in real time using WebSockets, allowing operators to follow
playbook execution and quickly see verification results (\emph{The
WebSocket API (WebSockets) - Web APIs \textbar{} MDN}, n.d.).

Finally, safety and governance are built into the implementation.
Generated playbooks are treated as suggestions for review rather than
directly trusted output. In the current workflow, they pass through
clarification, review, check-mode handling where supported, approval and
only then controlled deployment. Credentials are handled through device
inventory records and Ansible Vault support while execution results and
approval decisions remain visible through the platform. Overall, the
implementation is designed as an LLM-assisted, Ansible-centered,
approval-governed network automation and operations platform that is
demonstrable in a laboratory setting without overstating capabilities
such as full autonomous remediation or full controller-driven network
management as the current existing vendor platforms.

\textbf{3.3.4 Testing}

The testing phase of CogniShield is intended to verify that the current
implementation behaves safely and predictably before configuration
changes are sent to network devices. Because the platform combines
LLM-assisted playbook generation, approval-governed automation, topology
handling and monitoring support, the testing approach is layered rather
than relying on a single end-to-end check (Stojanov, 2025).

The first layer is unit testing. In the current implementation, pytest
is used to test important backend functions in isolation, including
intent parsing and clarification logic, context preparation,
NetBox-related processing, monitoring API behavior, dashboard-related
functions, onboarding flows and Ansible playbook generation helpers.
Where practical, these tests use mocked or stubbed dependencies so that
external systems such as NetBox, monitoring services or LLM providers do
not have to be live for every test run. This helps verify error handling
for cases such as missing parameters, ambiguous requests, incomplete
inventory data or invalid generated output.

The next layer is integration testing, which checks how multiple
subsystems work together. For the current project, this is done using
the application stack and supporting lab services that are actually used
in development, such as the FastAPI backend, the React frontend, the
SQLite-based application data layer, the ChromaDB-backed knowledge store
and the InfluxDB/Telegraf monitoring path. NetBox, the remote Linux
Ansible runner and EVE-NG are then connected as external lab services
where required. Integration testing focuses on realistic workflows such
as inventory synchronization, planned-versus-live topology handling,
knowledge-base rebuild, prompt preview and clarification, playbook
generation, check-mode execution, approval submission and deployment job
handling. Negative testing is also included so that ambiguous requests,
missing required fields, unsupported combinations and unavailable
dependencies can be observed and handled more safely.

The final layer is system and acceptance testing in the laboratory
environment. In this stage, EVE-NG is used to provide a small
campus-style network for validating the operator-visible workflows of
the platform. Typical scenarios include viewing planned and live
topology, comparing both views, generating a playbook from a
natural-language request, reviewing the result, running the Check
workflow, submitting the playbook to the Approval Queue, deploying an
approved playbook through the execution workflow and reviewing
monitoring

information through the Performance page. Manual browser-based workflow
testing is also important in the current implementation because many key
interactions, such as clarification prompts, inventory selection and
approval decisions, are UI-driven. Overall, the testing phase is
designed to confirm that the delivered platform works reliably within
its current scope as an LLM-assisted, approval-governed network
automation system rather than as a fully autonomous controller.

\textbf{3.4 Technology Deployed and Hardware}

The implementation of CogniShield is designed to be reproducible in a
university laboratory while remaining modular enough for future
extension. In the current implementation, the platform is built around a
Python-based backend, a React + TypeScript frontend and a set of
supporting services for monitoring, planned inventory support,
knowledge-base support and Ansible execution. The system is deployed in
a lab-oriented distributed environment using Proxmox-hosted virtual
machines and direct service execution, rather than as a production-scale
platform. This keeps the project practical for demonstration while
remaining aligned with its MVP scope.

\textbf{3.4.1 Languages, Frameworks and Core Libraries}

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Backend:} The backend is implemented in Python
using FastAPI and Uvicorn. This provides REST-style APIs, asynchronous
request handling and WebSocket support for live updates and
execution-related status visibility.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Frontend:} The frontend is implemented as a React
+ TypeScript + Vite single-page application. Styling and UI composition
are supported by Tailwind CSS and DaisyUI while the interface is
organized into dedicated views such as topology, device inventory,
Ansible Studio, approval queue, onboarding, configuration backup,
knowledge base and performance monitoring.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Visualization and UI components:} D3.js is used
for topology visualization, Monaco Editor is used for playbook and
template editing, Recharts is used for performance charts and xterm.js
is used for the SSH terminal component.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{LLM and knowledge support:} The current
implementation supports Gemini and Ollama as LLM providers. For
knowledge-grounded support, the project uses a ChromaDB-backed knowledge
store together with curated knowledge-base documents and Ansible
reference material. This is used to support bounded retrieval for
playbook generation and related workflows.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Automation and execution:} Ansible Core and
ansible-runner are used for check and deployment workflows. Because
Ansible control-node support is limited on Windows, the current
implementation also supports a remote Linux Ansible runner so that the

backend can delegate execution to a Linux VM without changing the
frontend workflow.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Network integration:} pynetbox is used for NetBox
integration, Paramiko-based vendor drivers are used for SSH-based
topology discovery and requests-based API integration is used where
external HTTP services are involved. Additional Ansible-related
libraries such as netmiko and selected NETCONF-related tooling are
included for specific execution or fallback scenarios where applicable.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Monitoring stack:} The current monitoring workflow
primarily uses InfluxDB for time-series metrics storage and Telegraf for
SNMP-based collection. Prometheus and Grafana can also be integrated in
the wider lab environment where available.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Storage:} The current implementation uses SQLite
as the main relational application data store for local application and
workflow records. NetBox is used as the planned inventory and topology
reference source where applicable while ChromaDB provides persistent
vector-store support for the knowledge layer.

●\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Virtualization and lab environment:} Proxmox is
used as the virtualization platform for the wider lab setup while EVE-NG
is used to simulate the campus-style network topology that CogniShield
interacts with during testing and demonstration.

\textbf{3.4.2 Hardware and Deployment Considerations}

The current implementation is developed as a minimum viable product
(MVP) for a student laboratory environment. It is not intended to match
the deployment scale, resilience or operational maturity of enterprise
platforms such as Cisco Catalyst Center, Huawei iMaster NCE or Juniper
Mist. Instead, it is designed as a practical and demonstrable research
prototype that integrates selected automation, topology, monitoring and
AI-assisted workflow capabilities within a constrained lab setup due to
limited development hardware resources and development time.

The project is deployed on a Proxmox server hosting four virtual
machines, with a total of 32 GB RAM shared across all virtual machines.
In the current setup:

●\hspace{0pt} VM 1 (192.168.1.249) hosts EVE-NG, which provides the
simulated campus network topology and virtual network devices.

●\hspace{0pt} VM 2 (192.168.1.250) hosts the telemetry-related services,
including InfluxDB, Telegraf used in the lab environment.

●\hspace{0pt} VM 3 (192.168.1.251) hosts the Linux-based remote Ansible
runner and the local Ollama runtime, which is configured in the current
implementation to use Qwen 2.5 Coder 3B by default and can be used for
other small local models where resources allow.

●\hspace{0pt} VM 4 (192.168.1.252) hosts NetBox, together with
Prometheus and Grafana for supporting inventory and monitoring-related
functions.

The main CogniShield application itself is then operated in a direct
lab-oriented manner from the main machine environment, rather than
through a more complex deployment model. This matches the current
implementation more accurately and keeps the MVP easier to run,
troubleshoot and demonstrate.

From a hardware perspective, the largest resource demand usually comes
from the EVE-NG virtual network lab and the number of active virtual
machines rather than from the frontend or backend web application alone.
The local AI-hosting setup is also constrained by the available
hardware, with approximately 4 GB VRAM available for the Ollama-based
local LLM environment. As a result, the current implementation is better
suited to smaller local models, such as the configured Qwen model,
rather than large on-premise foundation models. These hardware limits
are consistent with the MVP nature of the project and help define the

practical scope of the current implementation.

\textbf{3.4.3 Security, Privacy and Safe-By-Design Choices}

The current implementation follows a human-governed and safety-oriented
approach. Generated playbooks are not treated as commands to be applied
automatically without review. Instead, the workflow keeps the operator
involved through request clarification where needed, generated playbook
review, Check or dry-run handling where supported and approval-based
deployment before live execution. This is an important boundary in the
current system and should be stated clearly to avoid overstating the
level of autonomy.

For access control, the application includes authentication and
role-based workflow control so that sensitive actions such as approval,
rejection and deployment are restricted to authorized users. In the
laboratory setting, device access is intended to use only the
permissions necessary for testing and controlled configuration tasks.

For secrets and credentials, the current implementation uses a
combination of environment-based configuration, device inventory
credentials and Ansible Vault support. This is more accurate than
describing the system as using a full centralized secrets-management
platform in its present form. Audit-related information such as
generated playbooks, approval decisions, execution results and
timestamps is also retained in the platform so that actions remain
reviewable during testing and demonstration.

\textbf{3.5 SDG Alignment}

CogniShield is primarily aligned with Sustainable Development Goal 9
(Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure). In its current form, the
project demonstrates how a lab-scale, open and modular platform can
support innovation in network operations through the combination of
automation, monitoring, planned inventory support and LLM-assisted
workflow guidance. Rather than targeting enterprise-scale autonomy, the
current implementation contributes as a practical prototype that
explores how intelligent and governed automation can be applied in a
realistic academic environment.

This alignment is especially relevant from an educational and innovation
perspective. Because the platform is implemented using accessible
technologies and open development practices, it can support students,
researchers and educators in learning about network automation, topology
management, Ansible-based operations and AI-assisted workflows in a more
hands-on way. In that sense, the project contributes to innovation
capacity-building rather than only to a finished commercial product.

The project also has potential relevance to smaller organizations such
as SMEs and startups, particularly in showing how approval-governed
automation and low-cost virtualized lab infrastructure can be used to
study more efficient network operations. However, this contribution
should be understood within the limits of the current implementation.
CogniShield is best positioned as a research-oriented MVP that supports
experimentation, learning and future extension, rather than as a direct
replacement for mature enterprise network management platforms.

\textbf{3.6 Conclusion \& Future Works}

In conclusion, this chapter has presented the requirements, design
direction, implementation approach, testing strategy and deployment
considerations for CogniShield in a way that is aligned with the current
existing implementation. The project is positioned not as a full
autonomous controller or enterprise-equivalent platform but as an
LLM-assisted, Ansible-centered, approval-governed network automation and
operations MVP for a university laboratory setting. This clearer scope
is important because it keeps the report technically defensible while
still showing the novelty and practical value of the system.

\textbf{Architecture and Implementation Guidelines}

The architecture described in this chapter also shown that the current
architecture is built around a practical combination of FastAPI, React,
NetBox-linked planned inventory support, live topology discovery,
Ansible execution workflow, knowledge-base-assisted generation and
monitoring integration within a Proxmox-based virtual lab. The use of
EVE-NG, separate service virtual machines and a remote Linux Ansible
runner helps provide a realistic but manageable environment for
demonstration and testing. This makes the project suitable for
controlled experimentation without overstating it as a production-ready
orchestration platform.

\textbf{Testing Strategy and Hardware Recommendations}

The testing and deployment discussions further reinforce the project's
MVP nature. By combining unit testing, integration testing, manual
workflow validation and lab-based system testing, the project aims to
confirm that the main operator-visible workflows behave safely and
predictably. The hardware and virtualization choices also reflect real
student-lab constraints, including the use of four Proxmox virtual
machines, shared memory resources and a small local LLM runtime.

\textbf{Future Works and Project Progression}

For future work, the most meaningful next steps are to improve the
reliability and speed of the intent parsing and clarification workflow,
expand multi-vendor reference coverage, strengthen monitoring and
topology-data consistency and refine the approval and execution pipeline
based on further testing. Additional work could also improve local-model
performance under limited hardware, enhance user evaluation of the
interface and workflow clarity and further mature modules such as
onboarding, backup or governance-related support where appropriate. By
progressing in this measured way, CogniShield can continue to develop as
a useful research and educational platform for governed AI-assisted
network automation without overstating its current level of maturity.
